# static Keyword


`static` means the member belongs to the class, not the object.


##  static Variable

A variable shared among all objects of a class.


### Example

class Test {
    static int count = 0;
}


👉 Only one copy exists



## static Method

A method that can be called without creating an object


###  Example

class Test {
    static void show() {
        System.out.println("Static method");
    }
}
```

Usage:
Test.show();



## static Block

Executed once when class is loaded


###  Example

static {
    System.out.println("Static block executed");
}




## 🔹 Important Points

* Static members belong to class
* Access using class name
* Cannot directly access non-static members



# final Keyword

`final` means constant / cannot be changed


##  final Variable

Value cannot be modified after assignment


### Example

final int x = 10;
// x = 20; ❌ error



##  final Method

Cannot be overridden



### Example

class Parent {
    final void show() {}
}

##  final Class

Cannot be inherited



### Example
final class A {}
// class B extends A {} ❌ error




#  static vs final

| Feature  | static               | final           |
| -------- | -------------------- | --------------- |
| Meaning  | Belongs to class     | Cannot change   |
| Variable | Shared among objects | Constant        |
| Method   | No object needed     | Cannot override |
| Class    | Not applicable       | Cannot extend   |



#  Best Practices

* Use `static` for shared data
* Use `final` for constants
* Use `static final` for constants

# Constructors cannot be static because static members belong to the class,
while constructors are used to initialize objects.
Static Block can be closest alternative

# A class can be final but not static (top-level)
 Static is allowed only for nested classes


##  Example
static final double PI = 3.14;

👉 Constant + shared
